{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/289055-wtf2ed-questions-about-antagonists-and-their-behaviour","id":289055,"title":"[WtF2ed] Questions about Antagonists and their Behaviour","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-21T16:00:06-07:00","id":289055,"title":"[WtF2ed] Questions about Antagonists and their Behaviour","content":"Death Rage is a powerful thing in 2ed, and from reading spoiled Merits, Rites and Gifts, it seems Uratha are very well equipped to hunt down and best their prey.<br /> <br /> Putting aside troupes of mortal hunters who use silver when the Werewolf resistance party shows up to cause shinanigans, what kinds of behaviours and actions would the usual line up of Werewolf Antagonists take to out wit and match their hunters, or become the hunter themselves?<br /> <br /> What I'm thinking is, what does a Spirit do when it finds out it's under attack by a pack? How does it out think, trick or best them? What happens if the pack uses Gauru for six turns? Does it play keep away? Trick them into a pit?<br /> <br /> Do Pure go for straight fights breaking out the Gauru when the player pack does, or pick on the 'weakest' wolf? What tactics do the Hosts or Claimed take to best their foes?<br /> <br /> Werewolf is about hunting, and having good Antagonists means hunts don't go as expected, perhaps not always off the rails, but there should always be something a bit unexpected. I'm trying to think of some before 2ed releases later this year, and I'm asking the community for brain storming help and advice.<br /> <br /> How do we make Werewolf Antagonists unpredictable or dangerous in hunts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4134-gilbod","name":"GilboD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-21T17:01:03-07:00","id":289103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GilboD</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/289055-wtf2ed-questions-about-antagonists-and-their-behaviour#post289055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I'm thinking is, what does a Spirit do when it finds out it's under attack by a pack? How does it out think, trick or best them? What happens if the pack uses Gauru for six turns? Does it play keep away? Trick them into a pit?<br /> <br /> Do Pure go for straight fights breaking out the Gauru when the player pack does, or pick on the 'weakest' wolf? What tactics do the Hosts or Claimed take to best their foes?<br /> <br /> Werewolf is about hunting, and having good Antagonists means hunts don't go as expected, perhaps not always off the rails, but there should always be something a bit unexpected. I'm trying to think of some before 2ed releases later this year, and I'm asking the community for brain storming help and advice.<br /> <br /> How do we make Werewolf Antagonists unpredictable or dangerous in hunts?</div> </div> </div> </div>For spirits, it honestly depends on the rank and type of spirit. A rank 1 spirit may have limited intelligence and simply keep on the same target regardless of other threats, while a rank 3+ could have significant cunning to realize the werewolf in the back is the ringleader and a non-fighter and take advantage of the fact to quickly destroy them or put them in a bad position.<br /> <br /> Similarly, the spirit if confronted with an werewolf in Gauru form which is healing damage faster than taken, the spirit could run, it could switch targets, or use a numina to summon allies.<br /> <br /> For Pure, I assume they have intelligence and cunning equal to that of their Forsaken counterparts and can go for a brute force method, a pickoff the weakest tactic, or even depending on their specialties, blow them up with high explosives (Science is such an underutilized skill)<br /> <br /> Hosts can be very dangerous indeed and are used to being hunted. I would not be surprised at all if a Beshilu or Azlu acted as bait to lure Werewolves into a trap and swarm them.<br /> <br /> My personal favorite tactic however is let the Werewolf players think one thing even if they are wrong. That spirit that is all friendly to them? Spy. That cowardly Azlu? Bait. The Pure at the edge of the territory? Specializes in political warfare and makes Forsaken lives miserable because of it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/444-dessembrae","name":"Dessembrae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar444_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-21T22:28:48-07:00","id":289262,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/206414-tactics-for-hunting\">Here's a link to a relevant thread</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-22T09:28:25-07:00","id":289666,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Christian A</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/289055-wtf2ed-questions-about-antagonists-and-their-behaviour?p=289262#post289262\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/206414-tactics-for-hunting\">Here's a link to a relevant thread</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very cool. I'm trying to think in reverse of this.<br /> <br /> When the Forsaken come a knockin' with one of these tactics, how does the Antagonist respond? How has it prepared for some of these eventualities?<br /> <br /> More to the point, how have fellow Storytellers surprised their Werewolf Players with Antagonists during hunts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4134-gilbod","name":"GilboD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-22T12:13:28-07:00","id":289870,"title":"","content":"Might be good to have a timeline for your more proactive NPCs to follow, rather than just having them &quot;float&quot; around, waiting for the PCs to happen. For example:<br /> <br /> &quot;Day 1, prior to any contact with the PCs, this certain Pure pack will be hunting down Gafflings for their totem to eat, because their pack Aspiration is for the totem to grow almighty in power. Day 2 and Day 3, if their operations continue to go unmolested, they'll stir up some activity Skinside to attract the presence of more &quot;prey&quot; spirits so they don't exhaust the supply. Day 4, they resume hunting operations, and Day 5, they rest and lick any wounds they might have incurred during their activities.&quot;<br /> <br /> Most GMs, when presented with their players' desire to go on the hunt for something, often make the players begin with identifying and/or finding the prey they wish to hunt, following up with a chase/ambush scene to bring it to heel, and then a fight/negotiation if said prey is capable of exercising either option. This is all well and good when the prey isn't proactively defending itself from the PCs' predation or, even better, also hunting them down; it's basically a moving target, so all the PCs have to do is search and destroy. But when the prey decides to be predator, it then becomes a game of move vs move, like chess. Simulating the Pure/Jaggling/Hunters/whatever having minds of their own, having <i>agendas</i> of their own and having operations of their own, will tell you what you need to know when the PCs come knocking at their door, or vice versa. Consider:<br /> <br /> &quot;Day 1: The Covenant (an NPC pack of Fire-Touched) was hunting forest Gafflings to feed their totem, the Burning Angel. The Graybacks (PC Forsaken pack) caught wind of their activities and gave chase. The Graybacks emerge victorious, but they honor <i>Imru Nu Fir Imru</i> in the fight and let the Covenant wolves go, shamed but alive. As expected, however, the Covenant vows revenge.&quot;<br /> <br /> Day 2: The Covenant, after a night of recovering and planning, decide to learn more about these Graybacks. Their ritemaster calls some information Gafflings, which they bribe to find out the deed-names of the Graybacks and their haunts. Then their pathfinder (just because they don't have Auspices doesn't mean that the Pure can't have specialists now, does it?) goes on the prowl, scouting out the Graybacks' homes as revealed by the Gaffies to confirm the information. She doesn't stay overly long, however; all she needs is a few signs of Uratha activity in one area, and then she moves on to the others. All in all, this may take up to a day or two.<br /> <br /> Of course, if the Graybacks keep a particularly vigilant watch over their territory, then the Covenant's pathfinder may be in for a shock.<br /> <br /> Day 3: Let's say the Covenant's pathfinder didn't get caught, and let's further postulate that she got lucky, and managed to find the youngest and weakest of the Graybacks first. What happens next? Well, it depends on the personal preferences of the pathfinder in question. It's likely that many would do &quot;just as the alpha ordered&quot; and go back to their packs to spill their findings, but maybe the Covenant has been cursed with a pathfinder who's eager to prove herself worthy of Rabid Wolf's blessing; she decides to &quot;test&quot; the weakest of the Graybacks, ambushing him while he's alone. Too early to be sinning against Harmony just yet, though, so if she wins this one, she won't kill but most likely will cripple the pup, thus weakening the Graybacks as a whole. If the pup proves to be stronger than expected, though, she'll do her best to get away.<br /> <br /> Let's say that the dice favors the Grayback pup this time, but the Covenant's pathfinder makes her escape. She goes for their ritemaster to get Rite of Cleansed Scent done on her; it wouldn't do to leave a trail for the Graybacks to follow so easily, after all. Then she meets with the rest of the pack, and after punishing the pathfinder for her arrogance, they decide that it's much too risky to try and take the Graybacks head-on at this point; now they'll be prepared and on guard. However, the Covenant remembers a nearby nest of Beshilu that they once encountered during a hunt. Maybe they could manipulate the Beshilu into swarming the Graybacks' territory?&quot;<br /> <br /> tl;dr: Give your NPCs minds of their own, agendas of their own, plans of action of their own, if they're the kind that are willing and capable of playing the PCs at their own game. Make them react to the PCs' actions and change their plans accordingly. And don't be afraid to play to your NPCs' weaknesses as much as you play to their strengths. Don't be afraid to let them make mistakes and fail. The Wolf Must Hunt, hunters take advantage of weaknesses and failures, and your players will love you for letting them play the hunters their characters could be. Or... they make the mistake of taking their enemies too lightly (why would <i>Uratha</i> do that, though?), and they get aced. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3702-ursufgutha-storm-born","name":"Ursufgutha Storm-Born","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}